#!/usr/bin/env bash
# The admin step of taking a repository in; Colly runs it from this clone, on the Human's gh login, first without
# --apply, then with it after his one approval of the changes it prints; each successful run without --merge ends
# with his manual Linear Code installation step:
#   bin/onboard-owner <owner/name> --id <repository id>            show what it would change, and change nothing
#   bin/onboard-owner <owner/name> --id <repository id> --apply    invite the agents' account (write), set the
#        factory's rulesets (forcePush defaults to pstack: no rewrite on main or claims; on main, a bootstrap one until the kit lands, then the kit's
#        gate, which requires verify and tier: no reviewer runs on Actions, FAC-100), allow auto-merge (#139), once the
#        kit is on main set the Actions event policy "factory gate: pull_request_target" (#337: GitHub blocks pull_request_target
#        in a public repository from 2026-12-02 unless a policy allows it), and read it all back. The policy names the workflow
#        files on main that run on pull_request_target and allows them every event their on: block names, read from those
#        files; a file whose on: block is not in the kit's form stops the run. onboard-doctor reports the policy missing.
#   bin/onboard-owner <his login>/factory --id <id> [--apply]   the factory's own repository: only that policy, for the
#        workflows on its main that run on pull_request_target; its own ruleset stays, and no kit ruleset goes there
#   It sets none of the gate's settings (#335): the three runners are in the workflows on main, which bin/onboard fills in
#   (--runner, --pr-runner, --trusted-runner) and a pull request the Human approves changes. The old options are refused,
#   with nothing done. A main still on the kit before FAC-100 whose gate-trusted.yml names the reviewer entire (#155) keeps
#   the gate that requires verify, tier and Entire's check Entire Gates pinned to its App, until its kit update (FAC-102).
#   bin/onboard-owner <owner/name> --id <repository id> --merge all|1-2|none [--apply]
#        moves the repository's merge switch (#333): the two review parameters of the gate ruleset. The run writes the live
#        ruleset back with those two changed and nothing else in it (whatever else it holds stays as it is), and no other
#        setting: any other difference from the kit is listed "not changed" and left; a gate not yet in force, or with no
#        pull_request rule, is refused. Without --apply it shows the two parameters, from and to, and makes no change; with
#        it, it reads the two parameters from the ruleset and the position from the rules in force. all: every pull request
#        merges by itself once its checks are green, tier 3 included; 1-2: tier 1 and 2 do, and tier 3 waits for the
#        Human's approval (the default of a new gate); none: every pull request waits for an approval. It runs under the
#        Human's login only and is asked before each move. Without --merge a run never changes the two parameters: a
#        changed gate ruleset is put back with the ones it holds. Every run names the position it reads once the kit is
#        on the branch. GitHub applies a move at once to the open pull requests, and the queue re-runs the `tier` check
#        of a tier-3 one whose recorded position and the one in force differ in a way that changes its verdict.
# The id is the repository's own (gh api repos/<owner>/<name> --jq .id), so a typo or a renamed repository is refused.
# Colly runs it with --apply again once the kit pull request has merged, to put the kit's gate in; his approval of
# the kit pull request covers that. It never runs git or anything the repository holds. FACTORY_AGENT (default:
# nami9288) names the agents' account.
# With --apply it sets factory-branches (main and claims: no rewrite; every branch with forcePush off), factory-tags (every tag: no create, update or delete, but
# for a repository admin), factory-claims (refs/heads/claim/**: no deletion, no rewrite, no bypass) and factory-bootstrap
# (main: a pull request, one approval of the last push, merge commits only), turns on "Allow auto-merge", and reads it all
# back; it calls only gh api and gh variable list. Once the agents' account has
# accepted the invitation, the team that claimed the issue runs bin/onboard <repo-url> from this root, logged into gh as that
# account.
exec node "$(dirname "$0")/lib/owner.mjs" "$@"
